---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="The Frontier"
  eyebrow="Otočac, Šumećica, Prozor"
  dek="One Latin word in an 1834 baptismal entry — Confiniarius — puts Hedviga's grandmother's people inside the Habsburg Military Frontier, and changes what kind of family they were.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The <a href={u("/zubrinic")}>Žubrinići</a> are the rarest name in this archive
    and, until the Otočac register was read, the least explained. The family tree gives Tereza
    Žubrinić one occupation — <em>domaćica</em>, housewife — and her father and grandfather none at
    all. The register gives the household a legal status instead.</p>
    <blockquote class="note">1834, <em>Januarii die 24ᵃ, Ottocsatii</em> — <strong>Nicolaus</strong>,
    <em>filius legitimus, Catholicus</em>. <em>Conditio:</em>
    <strong><em>Confiniarius, ex Pago Shumechia N⁰ 24</em></strong>. Parents
    <strong>Michaël <u>XUBRINICH</u></strong> and <em>Maria uxor ejus nata Oreskovich</em>.</blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What a <em>Confiniarius</em> was</h2>
    <p>The <strong>Military Frontier</strong> — <em>Vojna krajina</em>, <em>Militärgrenze</em> — was
    a belt of territory the Habsburgs administered <strong>not as a province but as a standing
    army</strong>. A frontiersman held his land from the Emperor on condition of hereditary military
    service. He was not a serf and not quite a free peasant: he was a soldier who farmed, in a
    household that owed a man to the regiment.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Otočac</strong> had been a Frontier captaincy since
    <strong>1553</strong>. In <strong>1619</strong> a body of <a href={u("/senj")}>Uskoks displaced
    from Senj</a> moved inland to Otočac and built the <strong>Fortica</strong> — so the two towns
    at the two ends of this family's map are joined by the same dispersal. Under
    <strong>Maria Theresa</strong> Otočac was raised to the headquarters of a regiment: one of the
    four of the <strong>Karlovac Generalate</strong>, beside Ogulin, Slunj and Lika.</p>
    <div class="note">So when the priest writes <em>Confiniarius</em> in 1834 he is not describing a
    job. He is recording which <strong>legal order</strong> the household belonged to — and by then
    the Otočac Regiment had existed for some seventy years. The Frontier was not abolished until
    <strong>1881</strong>, within Tereza's own lifetime.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Three Žubrinić households, three offices</h2>
    <p class="lede">The 1834 entry names three of them in four lines, and every one holds a post.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>The father</th><td><strong>Michaël Xubrinich</strong> — <em>Confiniarius</em>, of
        <strong>Šumećica no. 24</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>The godfather</th><td><strong>Petrus, son of Franciscus Xubrinich</strong> —
        <em>Vigiliarum Ma[gister]</em>, <strong>master of the watch</strong>, and
        <strong><em>pensionatus</em></strong>: a man drawing a pension, which means a career and a
        rank</td></tr>
      <tr><th>The godmother</th><td><strong>Maria, wife of Joannes Xubrinich</strong>,
        <strong><em>Silvarum Custos in Shumechia</em></strong> — <strong>forest warden</strong> of
        Šumećica</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">And the forestry is not a one-off. The tree records exactly one
    occupation across all <strong>422</strong> of its Žubrinići, and it is
    <strong>“Forest Ranger”</strong> — held by
    <a href={u("/people/joannes-juan-ivan-ive-zubrinic")}><strong>Joannes Žubrinić, born 19 June
    1837</strong></a>, three years after the entry above. Two generations, the same woodland
    office.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>Why forests mattered here.</strong> Frontier woodland was
    <strong>military property</strong>: the timber was the Empire's, and guarding it was a
    salaried, uniformed, appointed post — not agricultural work. A <em>Silvarum Custos</em> is a
    small official of the state.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The villages</h2>
    <div class="grid three">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Otočac</h3>
        <p>The regimental town in the <strong>Gacka</strong> valley, inland from Senj across the
        Velebit. <strong>53 people</strong> in this tree are born or die here — the single largest
        Žubrinić place.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Šumećica</h3>
        <p>Two house numbers, two centuries apart in the record:
        <a href={u("/people/toma-zubrinic")}><strong>Toma Žubrinić</strong></a>, born
        <strong>1768</strong> at <strong>Šumećica 2</strong>, and Michaël's household at
        <strong>Šumećica 24</strong> in 1834. The name is from <em>šuma</em> — <strong>forest</strong>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Prozor</h3>
        <p>Five kilometres south-east of Otočac, on the site of Roman <strong>Arupium</strong>.
        <strong>Seven children of one household</strong> are registered at <strong>Prozor 50</strong>
        between <strong>1858 and 1874</strong> — Nikola, Kata, Georgius, Magdalena, Mate, Ivan,
        Franjo, to Michael Žubrinić and Marija Rogić.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Following the rarest name out of Croatia</div>
    <h2>The line that reached Port Pirie</h2>
    <p class="lede">The <a href={u("/port-pirie")}>Australian Zubrinichs</a> can now be traced back
    three generations from the emigrant — and their story turns out to be the forestry family.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th><strong>Casparus</strong><br /><span class="dim">1767–1829</span></th>
        <td>“Kasper / Gaspar Žubrinić Xubrinich”, born 14 January 1767; married
        <strong>Helena</strong> (1765–1831), herself recorded under the surname Žubrinić</td></tr>
      <tr><th><strong>Elias “Ilija”</strong><br /><span class="dim">1806–1845</span></th>
        <td>Born 10 July 1806; married <strong>Ana Untersinger</strong> (1807–1845) —
        <strong>a German surname in Lika</strong>, which the Frontier's officer and administrative
        class would explain. <strong>Both died in 1845</strong>, leaving seven children between
        about one and fifteen.</td></tr>
      <tr><th><strong>Joannes</strong><br /><span class="dim">b. 19 June 1837</span></th>
        <td><strong>Forest Ranger.</strong> Eight years old when his parents died. Married
        <strong>Kata Brajković</strong> on 12 January 1859</td></tr>
      <tr><th><strong>Ivan / John</strong><br /><span class="dim">1860–1930</span></th>
        <td>Born the year after that wedding; reached <strong>Port Pirie, South Australia</strong>
        about 1884, and from his first child in 1885 every descendant is registered
        <strong>Zubrinich</strong>. <a href={u("/port-pirie")}>Port Pirie →</a></td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="note"><strong>Both parents in one year.</strong> Elias and Ana Untersinger are both
    given 1845. Two adults of thirty-eight and thirty-nine dying in the same year, in Lika, in the
    1840s, suggests an epidemic or a famine rather than coincidence — but the tree gives no dates,
    no causes and no source, and this archive has not looked.
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">A fourth office, and a fourth Žubrinić</div>
    <h2>The foresters keep appearing</h2>
    <p class="lede">The Otočac baptismal register for <strong>1835</strong> — the next book along —
    produces another one. Standing as godfather at an entry that September is
    <strong>Stephanus Xubrinich</strong>, entered as of the <em>Centuria Ottocsana</em> and
    <strong><em>Sylvarum Custos</em></strong>.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>1834</th><td><strong>Joannes Xubrinich</strong>, <em>Silvarum Custos in
        Shumechia</em></td></tr>
      <tr><th>1835</th><td><strong>Stephanus Xubrinich</strong>, of the Otočac company,
        <em>Sylvarum Custos</em></td></tr>
      <tr><th>b. 1837</th><td><a href={u("/people/joannes-juan-ivan-ive-zubrinic")}><strong>Joannes
        Žubrinić</strong></a> — <strong>“Forest Ranger”</strong>, the only occupation the tree
        records for any of its 422 Žubrinići</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>And a fifth office, from 1845.</strong> The godmother at
    the baptism of Josephus Žubrinić on 16 March 1845 is entered as
    <strong>Maria Žubrinić, <em>mulier Custodis Carcerum</em></strong> — wife of the
    <strong>keeper of the gaol</strong>. Not a forester this time, but the same thing in kind: a
    salaried post in the Frontier administration, held by a Žubrinić, recorded only because
    somebody stood as a godparent.</p>
    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <h3>Six posts, four generations, one surname — this is what the family did</h3>
      <table class="reg">
        <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Who</th><th>Post</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">1834</td><td>Petrus, son of Franciscus</td>
            <td><em>Vigiliarum Magister pensionatus</em> — <strong>retired master of the
              watch</strong>, drawing a pension</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">1834</td><td>Joannes</td>
            <td><em>Sylvarum Custos in Shumechicza</em> — <strong>forest guard</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">1835</td><td>Stephanus</td>
            <td><em>Sylvarum Custos</em> of the <em>Centuria Ottocsana</em> — <strong>forest guard
              of the Otočac company</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">1845</td><td>the wife of a Žubrinić</td>
            <td><em>mulier Custodis Carcerum</em> — <strong>wife of the keeper of the
              gaol</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">b. 1837</td><td><a href={u("/people/joannes-juan-ivan-ive-zubrinic")}>Joannes</a></td>
            <td><strong>Forest Ranger</strong> — the only occupation the tree records for any
              Žubrinić at all</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">1910</td><td><a href={u("/people/nikola-zubrinic-xubrinic")}>Nikola</a></td>
            <td><em>kontrolor gradske daće</em> — <strong>controller of the municipal
              excise</strong>, at Senj</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Read down that column and it is not a run of
      coincidences. It is an occupation.</strong> Three of the six are forest posts, which is what
      a Frontier regiment needed on wooded karst and what it paid for. One is the watch. One is the
      gaol. And the last, <strong>1910</strong>, is twenty-nine years after the Military Frontier
      was abolished — the same family still in <em>salaried public service</em>, now municipal
      rather than Imperial.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>Which makes what happened to the other side of this
      descent sharper.</strong> While the Žubrinići were guarding forests and counting excise, the
      Blaževići at <a href={u("/blazevic")}>Smokvica 114</a> were <em>seljaci</em> and then
      <em>nadničari</em>, and the Papići went from <em>Dominus</em> at Karlobag to
      <em>nadničar</em> at Senj in three generations. <strong>Two of Hedviga's four families held
      office; two lost everything.</strong> They meet in her.</p>
      <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Every one of these six comes out of a
      <strong>godparent column or an occupation field</strong> — not one is recorded because
      somebody wrote the man's own life down. <a href={u("/searched")}>Six posts from about thirty
      pages read →</a></p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">Four men and one household of one rare surname, in one small
    district, holding salaried Imperial office across at least two generations.
    <strong>This is what the family did.</strong> The tree, which records none of it, gives Tereza
    Žubrinić one word — <em>domaćica</em>, housewife — and her forebears nothing.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">A correction to this archive's own direct line</div>
    <h2>The mother who was born after her son</h2>
    <p class="lede">This archive published
    <a href={u("/people/maria-catharina-oreskovic-oreskovic")}><strong>Maria Catharina
    Orešković</strong></a> as the wife of Michael Žubrinić and the mother of
    <a href={u("/people/nicolaus-zubrinic-xubrinic")}>Nicolaus</a>, with a birth date of
    <strong>8 September 1835</strong>. Nicolaus was born <strong>24 January 1834</strong>.
    <strong>She is twenty months younger than her son.</strong></p>
    <p>The document settles it rather than rescuing it. The scan attached to her in the tree is a
    real entry and it is <em>hers</em> — Otočac baptisms, 1835, entry 41:</p>
    <blockquote class="note">1835, <em>Septembris die 8ᵛᵃ, Ottocsitii</em> — <strong>Maria
    Catharina</strong>, <em>filia legitima, Catholica</em>. <em>Conditio:</em>
    <em>Confinarius ex Pago <strong>Dubrava N⁰ D. 29</strong></em>. Parents:
    <strong>Marcus Oreskovich, <u>adoptatus Sekula</u>, et Lucia uxor ejus nata Sekula</strong>.</blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">Her parents in that entry are <strong>exactly</strong> the two the
    tree gives her — <a href={u("/people/marcus-oreskovic-oreskovic")}>Marcus Orešković</a> and
    <a href={u("/people/lucia-sekula")}>Lucia Sekula</a>. So the transcription is right and the
    <em>attachment</em> is wrong: a real Maria Catharina Orešković of Dubrava has been married to the
    wrong man and given somebody else's child.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Nicolaus's own 1834 entry names his mother as
    <em>“Maria uxor ejus <strong>nata Oreskovich</strong>”</em>. So an Orešković she was — just an
    earlier one, born perhaps around 1810, and still unidentified.</p>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <tr><th class="warn">What this costs</th><td><strong>Seven published ancestors</strong> hang
        below this join — Maria Catharina herself, her parents Marcus and Lucia, and the four
        Sekulas above them. Those people are real and their records are real. <strong>What is not
        shown is that they belong to this family.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th class="warn">A second sign</th><td>The same household in the tree lists
        <strong>“Nikola Žubrinić, b. 1880”</strong> as a <em>sibling</em> of Nicolaus, born 1834 —
        a gap of forty-six years. Where one attachment has slipped, others usually have.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Left standing</th><td>The rest of the line is untouched: Michael, Nicolaus and Tereza
        are joined by the 1834 register and by the
        <a href={u("/marriage-1920")}>1920 marriage entry</a>.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="note"><strong>What would fix it.</strong> The <strong>marriage of Michael Žubrinić
    and a Maria Orešković</strong> in the Otočac registers, some time before 1834 — which would name
    the right woman and her father. Until then this archive marks the Orešković–Sekula branch
    <strong>Disputed</strong> and keeps it visible rather than deleting it, because a wrong join is
    itself a finding. <a href={u("/method")}>Method →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The honest state of it</div>
    <h2>422 records, 90 loose ends, and an alphabet</h2>
    <p class="lede">The Žubrinić material in this tree is <strong>not one family</strong>. It is
    <strong>422 records</strong> resolving into <strong>90 loose ends</strong> — 90
    people with no recorded Žubrinić parent — which means the research has gathered the name
    without yet joining it up.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>Our line</th><td>Descends from an <strong>unnamed “Žubrinić”</strong> node with
        <strong>731 descendants</strong> in the tree — the largest fragment — through
        <a href={u("/people/toma-zubrinic")}>Toma (1768, Šumećica 2)</a>,
        <a href={u("/people/michael-zubrinic-zubrinic-xubrinich")}>Michael (1811)</a>,
        <a href={u("/people/nicolaus-zubrinic-xubrinic")}>Nicolaus (1834)</a> and
        <a href={u("/people/tereza-zubrinic-zubrinic")}>Tereza (1867)</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>The Australian line</th><td>Hangs above Casparus on a node whose <em>name</em>, in the
        tree, is <strong class="mono">“Brothers of Casparus (It Seems) Žubrinić”</strong>. That is
        not a person. It is a researcher's working guess, written into the structure.</td></tr>
      <tr class="warn"><th>Therefore</th><td><strong>The two lines are not joined by anything.</strong>
        Casparus (b. 14 January 1767) and Toma (b. 1768) are born <strong>a year apart</strong>, in
        the same small Frontier district, into a surname held today by about
        <strong>312 people on earth</strong>. They are <em>very probably</em> close kin.
        <strong>Probably is not shown.</strong></td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="note"><strong>What would join them.</strong> The <strong>Otočac parish registers of
    the 1760s</strong>, for the baptisms of Casparus (14 January 1767) and Toma (1768) — two entries
    on nearby pages, which would either name one father or two.
    <a href={u("/method")}>Why the placeholder nodes are dropped from this build →</a></div>
  </section>
</Base>
